HackPack — Portable Network Security Platform
I call this project my "HackPack" because the entire system is designed to fit into a small sling bag and function as a portable cybersecurity and networking platform. The name and aesthetic are inspired by Watch Dogs 2, including the wolf-emblem keychain attached to the bag.
Beneath the aesthetic, the HackPack is a practical Linux-based networking project built around a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, GL.iNet travel router, battery bank, and Pixel 3 XL running Termux. The system is designed to provide a portable, controlled network environment while operating over potentially untrusted public Wi-Fi.
The travel router provides the primary network gateway and can route traffic through WireGuard or the Tor network, while the Raspberry Pi provides local services including Pi-hole for DNS filtering and network-level blocking. The Pixel 3 XL serves as the administrative terminal, allowing me to manage and interact with the system through Termux without requiring a laptop.
The entire platform is designed to be small enough to deploy casually in environments such as cafés, libraries, or while traveling. One of the project's goals is to make secure networking practical without carrying an obvious collection of networking equipment everywhere I go.
And, admittedly, there is another design goal: make the whole thing look more like an ordinary sling bag than a portable cybersecurity lab. The Watch Dogs-inspired aesthetic is mostly for fun, but it gives the project its own identity while keeping the actual hardware relatively inconspicuous.
